	RVM

RVM is a RISC-V system emulator. It implements RV64IMAFDC, the Sv39, Sv48 and
Sv57 page table formats, and enough virtio to boot an unmodified Debian kernel
and userland from a disk image. It runs on Linux and macOS and it builds as an
Android app.

It is C11 against libc and libm. There is nothing else to install.


Building
--------

	make

produces ./rvm, about 92 KB. You need a C11 compiler and GNU make.

	make test

builds and runs the test suite, 31 suites and 602 assertions.

	make lint
	make MODE=sanity test
	make format

are the -Werror syntax pass, an ASan and UBSan build, and clang-format. See
docs/BUILDING for every target, mode and variable.


Running
-------

A kernel, an initrd and a disk image are attached to every GitHub release, so
downloading them is the short way. To build them yourself:

	./tools/fetch_kernel.sh --out vmlinux
	sudo ./tools/mkrootfs.sh --out disk.img
	./tools/run.sh

That ends at a login prompt on ttyS0. mkrootfs.sh needs root, about 2 GB of
memory and a route to deb.debian.org.

rvm builds its own device tree at boot, so no DTB file is required. dtb/rvm.dts
is the same tree written out for editing, and make dtb compiles it if you want
to compare.

	./rvm --help

lists the options. The useful ones are -m for guest memory, -k for the kernel,
-d for the disk, --stats for counters and --trace-from for the instruction
trace.


Documentation
-------------

	PLAN			what is built, what is not, and in what order
	CHANGELOG		what changed
	CONTRIBUTING		how to send a patch
	SECURITY		how to report a guest escape
	docs/ARCHITECTURE	how the pieces fit together
	docs/BUILDING		build modes, targets, the Android APK, CI
	docs/DEBUGGING		tracing a guest that goes wrong


Android
-------

The app lives in android/. It is one Activity and three custom Views. There is
no AndroidX, no Material Components, no Compose and no WebView; the terminal is
drawn with Canvas and the graphics surface is a SurfaceView. The dependencies
block in android/app/build.gradle.kts is empty and should stay that way.

android/jni/CMakeLists.txt compiles src/ directly, so there is one copy of the
emulator and the desktop build cannot drift away from the phone build.

	cd android && gradle --no-daemon assembleDebug

There is no gradle wrapper jar in this repository, so ./gradlew does not exist.
Use your own Gradle 8.11.1, or let CI do the build. The release APK is about
150 KB and the workflow fails if it ever goes over 5 MB.


Continuous integration
----------------------

Four workflows run on every push to main: build, test, lint and android. A
fifth, release, runs on a v* tag and publishes the APK. The badges are
deliberately absent; look at the Actions tab.


