## Boot shell

The default cmdline is `console=ttyS0 earlycon=ns16550a,mmio32,0x10000000
root=/dev/vda rootwait rw` -- no `init=` anywhere. What decides whether you get
a shell is the initrd: `tools/mkinitrd.sh` builds a busybox initramfs whose /init
brings eth0 up through the built-in NAT and then, if /dev/vda has an /sbin/init,
hands the console over to it, so you land in the distro. Put `rvm.shell=1` on
--bootargs to keep the initramfs shell and skip the hand-off.

The APK ships that same initramfs as an asset, so on a phone Kernel and Disk is
all there is to pick.

Two more bootargs matter on a phone, and the app passes both.  `rvm.time=virtual`
makes `mtime` follow retired instructions instead of host wall clock (see
src/devices/clint.c), so a boot that is slow because the host is slow is not also
a guest that thinks its CPU is stuck.  `nosoftlockup` silences the kernel watchdog,
which on a device with a foreground app and a two gigabyte copy in progress
reports true things in the wrong order.

The app also has a machine line: menu -> Machine edits `ram=1536 cpu=1`, clamped
to 256-4096 MiB and remembered between runs, and the value reaches `vmCreate`.
After Boot the console prints the sizes the VM really got.

The app holds a `PARTIAL_WAKE_LOCK` while a VM is running: without one Android
stops scheduling the vCPU thread when the screen times out, and a boot that needs
tens of minutes of CPU on a phone simply halts mid-init, silently.

`Boot to shell` is a branch in `/init` inside the initramfs rather than something
the emulator implements, so an initrd copied out of an older release accepts the
flag and ignores it.  The app now reads the image it is about to boot, puts its own
copy in place when that image predates the feature, and prints the boot arguments
and the initrd's capability in the banner - which is what tells the two ways the
setting can do nothing apart from a single screenshot.

Adding `shell=1` to that line skips the disk's init scripts and hands the console
to its `/bin/sh` instead (the initramfs' `rvm.shell=root`), which is the difference
between a prompt in ten seconds and a silent minute or two on a phone: enough to
run apt, ping and dpkg, not enough to have any service running.

